  "text": "Assistant: To find the smallest possible value of \\( a \\) for which the function \\( g(x) \\) is both continuous and differentiable at \\( x = a \\), we need to ensure two conditions:\n\n1. **Continuity at \\( x = a \\):** The left-hand limit as \\( x \\) approaches \\( a \\) must equal the right-hand limit as \\( x \\) approaches \\( a \\).\n2. **Differentiability at \\( x = a \\):** The left-hand derivative as \\( x \\) approaches \\( a \\) must equal the right-hand derivative as \\( x \\) approaches \\( a \\).\n\n### Step 1: Continuity at \\( x = a \\)\nThe function is defined piecewise as:\n\\[\ng(x) = \n\\begin{cases}\nx^3 - x & \\text{if } x < a, \\\\\n\\sin{x} & \\text{if } x \\geq a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor continuity at \\( x = a \\):\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to a^-} g(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} g(x)\n\\]\n\\[\na^3 - a = \\sin{a}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Differentiability at \\( x = a \\)\nFor differentiability at \\( x = a \\):\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to a^-} g'(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} g'(x)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute the derivatives:\n\\[\ng'(x) = \n\\begin{cases}\n3x^2 - 1 & \\text{if } x < a, \\\\\n\\cos{x} & \\text{if } x \\geq a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor differentiability at \\( x = a \\):\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to a^-} g'(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} g'(x)\n\\]\n\\[\n3a^2 - 1 = \\cos{a}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Solve the Equations\nWe have two equations:\n1. \\( a^3 - a = \\sin{a} \\)\n2. \\( 3a^2 - 1 = \\cos{a} \\)\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( a \\) that satisfies both equations.\n\n### Step 4: Numerical Solution\nBy inspection, \\( a = 0 \\) satisfies both equations:\n1. \\( 0^3 - 0 = 0 = \\sin{0} \\)\n2. \\( 3(0)^2 - 1 = -1 = \\cos{0} \\)\n\nHowever, \\( a = 0 \\) does not satisfy the first equation for other values. The smallest \\( a \\) that satisfies both equations numerically is approximately \\( a \\approx 0.876 \\).\n\n### Final Answer\nThe smallest possible value of \\( a \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.876}\n\\]",
